window.docContents[117].resolve({"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___1___-Problems/Problem-6___1___6___-McKay-graph/#Chapter6___Problem6___1___6":{"contents":"Problem 6.1.6. Let G \\neq \\{1\\} be a finite subgroup of SU(2) and let V be the 2-dimensional representation of G coming from its embedding into SU(2). Let V_i, i \\in I, be all the irreducible representations of G. Let r_{ij} be the multiplicity of V_i in V \\otimes V_j.\n\n(a) Show that r_{ij} = r_{ji}.\n\n(b) The McKay graph of G, M(G), is the graph whose vertices are labeled by i \\in I, and i is connected to j by r_{ij} edges. Show that M(G) is connected. (Use Problem 4.12.10.)\n\n(c) Show that M(G) is an affine Dynkin diagram (one of the \"forbidden\" graphs in Problem 6.1.3). For this, show that the matrix a_{ij} = 2\\delta_{ij} - r_{ij} is positive semidefinite but not definite, and use Problem 6.1.3.\n\nHint: Let f = \\sum x_i \\chi_{V_i}, where \\chi_{V_i} are the characters of V_i. Show directly that ((2 - \\chi_V)f, f) \\geq 0. When is it equal to 0? Next, show\nthat M(G) has no self-loops by using the fact that if G is not cyclic, then G contains the central element -\\text{Id} \\in SU(2).\n\n(d) Which groups from Problem 4.12.8 correspond to which diagrams?\n\n(e) Using the McKay graph, find the dimensions of irreducible representations of all finite G \\subset SU(2) (namely, show that they are the numbers labeling the vertices of the affine Dynkin diagrams on our pictures). Compare with the results on subgroups of SO(3) we obtained in Problem 4.12.8.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t6. Quiver representations\t6.1. Problems\tProblem 6.1.6: McKay graph","header":"Problem 6.1.6: McKay graph","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___1___-Problems/Problem-6___1___6___-McKay-graph/#Chapter6___Problem6___1___6"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___1___-The-definition-of-a-category/AbelianGroups-as-full-subcategory-of-Groups/#Chapter7___Example7___1___5":{"contents":"Example 7.1.5. The category AbelianGroups is a full subcategory of the category Groups.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t7. Introduction to categories\t7.1. The definition of a category\tAbelianGroups as full subcategory of Groups","header":"AbelianGroups as full subcategory of Groups","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___1___-The-definition-of-a-category/AbelianGroups-as-full-subcategory-of-Groups/#Chapter7___Example7___1___5"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology/Section-7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology/#Chapter7___Introduction_7___8":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t7. Introduction to categories\t7.8. Complexes and cohomology\tSection 7.8: Complexes and cohomology","header":"Section 7.8: Complexes and cohomology","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology/Section-7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology/#Chapter7___Introduction_7___8"}});
